# Tech Stack

## 1. Approved Stack

The solution MUST use this stack. Introducing anything outside it requires a decision
(ADR/TDR) and an update to this rule.

| Concern | Choice |
|---|---|
| Language | TypeScript (strict mode) — extension host and webview share the language |
| Runtime | Node.js (VS Code extension host); webview runs in the browser sandbox |
| Delivery | VS Code extension (`package.json` `contributes`) |
| Graph rendering | `d3-force` (force simulation) drawn on an HTML `<canvas>` |
| Graph data model | Plain TypeScript interfaces (`Node`, `Edge`) — no graph-library coupling |
| Bundler | `esbuild` |
| Package manager | `npm` |
| Testing (domain layer) | `vitest` |
| Linting / formatting | `eslint` + `prettier` |

## 2. Target Environment

- Extension host targets the current stable VS Code engine version declared in
  `package.json` `engines.vscode`.
- The webview targets the embedded Chromium runtime provided by VS Code. It MUST NOT
  assume Node APIs are available inside the webview.

## 3. Constraints

- MUST NOT introduce a second graph library or a second bundler/package manager.
- The domain layer MUST NOT import `vscode` or any Node-only module (see
  architecture-design.mdc).
- MUST NOT hardcode theme colors or model endpoints; read them from the active VS Code
  theme (`--vscode-*` CSS variables) and configuration respectively.

## 4. Model & Markdown Integration

- Markdown link extraction (`[[wikilink]]`, `#tag`, `[label](path)`) is done by the
  domain `MarkdownParser` — pure, no framework imports.
- The graph is a projection of the parsed index; rendering details (canvas, d3) live
  only in the webview.

## Examples

### Correct

```ts
// domain/parseMarkdown.ts — pure, no vscode import
export function extractLinks(content: string): string[] {
  return [...content.matchAll(/\[\[([^\]]+)\]\]/g)].map((m) => m[1]);
}
```

### Incorrect — NEVER do these

- `import * as vscode from "vscode"` inside `src/domain/`.
- Adding `three.js` or `sigma` alongside `d3-force` without an ADR.
- `import * as fs from "fs"` inside the webview script.
